### What is the N-Queens problem?

Place n queens on an n-by-n chessboard so that no two queens attack each other. Queens attack along rows, columns, and both diagonals. Since we place exactly one queen per row, row conflicts are automatically avoided. The challenge is column and diagonal conflicts.

This is LeetCode 51 (return all boards) and LeetCode 52 (return count only).

```java
package com.bank.service;

// N=4 has 2 solutions:
// Solution 1:    Solution 2:
// .Q..           ..Q.
// ...Q           Q...
// Q...           ...Q
// ..Q.           .Q..
```

### How does one-queen-per-row shrink the search?

The naive approach would try every cell on the board. But since each row must contain exactly one queen, you only need to choose a column for each row. That reduces the problem from n-squared choices to at most n choices per row, which is at most n^n branches before pruning.

### How do the three constraint sets enable O(1) conflict checks?

Maintain three `HashSet<Integer>` values:
- `cols`: occupied columns.
- `diag1`: occupied `row - col` values (down-right diagonals).
- `diag2`: occupied `row + col` values (down-left diagonals).

Cells on the same down-right diagonal share `row - col`. Cells on the same down-left diagonal share `row + col`. A cell at (r, c) is safe exactly when `cols` does not contain c, `diag1` does not contain r - c, and `diag2` does not contain r + c.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class NQueensSolver {

    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        List<List<String>> solutions = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] queens = new int[n]; // queens[row] = col
        Set<Integer> cols = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Integer> diag1 = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Integer> diag2 = new HashSet<>();

        solve(n, 0, queens, cols, diag1, diag2, solutions);
        return solutions;
    }

    private void solve(int n, int row, int[] queens,
                       Set<Integer> cols, Set<Integer> diag1,
                       Set<Integer> diag2,
                       List<List<String>> solutions) {
        if (row == n) {
            solutions.add(buildBoard(queens, n));
            return;
        }
        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
            if (cols.contains(col) || diag1.contains(row - col)
                || diag2.contains(row + col)) {
                continue;
            }
            queens[row] = col;
            cols.add(col);
            diag1.add(row - col);
            diag2.add(row + col);

            solve(n, row + 1, queens, cols, diag1, diag2, solutions);

            cols.remove(col);
            diag1.remove(row - col);
            diag2.remove(row + col);
        }
    }

    private List<String> buildBoard(int[] queens, int n) {
        List<String> board = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            char[] line = new char[n];
            java.util.Arrays.fill(line, '.');
            line[queens[row]] = 'Q';
            board.add(new String(line));
        }
        return board;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NQueensSolver solver = new NQueensSolver();
        List<List<String>> solutions = solver.solveNQueens(4);
        System.out.println("4-Queens: " + solutions.size() + " solutions");
        for (List<String> sol : solutions) {
            sol.forEach(System.out::println);
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

