.Q..
```

### What about the bitmask optimization?

Replace the three sets with three integers. Use bitwise OR to mark conflicts and bitwise AND with complement to find free columns. The lowest set bit trick extracts one free column at a time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NQueensBitmask {
    private int count = 0;

    public int totalNQueens(int n) {
        count = 0;
        solve(n, 0, 0, 0, 0);
        return count;
    }

    private void solve(int n, int row, int cols, int d1, int d2) {
        if (row == n) { count++; return; }
        int free = ((1 << n) - 1) & ~(cols | d1 | d2);
        while (free != 0) {
            int bit = free & (-free);
            free ^= bit;
            solve(n, row + 1, cols | bit, (d1 | bit) << 1, (d2 | bit) >> 1);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NQueensBitmask solver = new NQueensBitmask();
        for (int n = 4; n <= 12; n++) {
            System.out.println("n=" + n + ": " + solver.totalNQueens(n) + " solutions");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
n=4: 2 solutions
n=5: 10 solutions
n=6: 4 solutions
n=7: 40 solutions
n=8: 92 solutions
n=9: 352 solutions
n=10: 724 solutions
n=11: 2680 solutions
n=12: 14200 solutions
```

### How does symmetry reduce the search?

The board has vertical symmetry. If a queen is placed in the left half of the first row, the mirrored placement in the right half produces a symmetric solution. By only trying columns 0 to (n-1)/2 in row 0 and doubling the count (adjusting for the center column when n is odd), you nearly halve the search.

### What are the edge cases?

- **n = 1**: one solution, a single queen on the only cell.
- **n = 2 and n = 3**: zero solutions; no valid placement exists.
- **n = 8**: the classic problem with 92 distinct solutions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NQueensEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NQueensSolver solver = new NQueensSolver();
        System.out.println("n=1: " + solver.solveNQueens(1).size() + " solutions");
        System.out.println("n=2: " + solver.solveNQueens(2).size() + " solutions");
        System.out.println("n=3: " + solver.solveNQueens(3).size() + " solutions");
    }
}
```

