n=1: 1 solutions
n=2: 0 solutions
n=3: 0 solutions
```

### What are common mistakes?

- Using O(n) board scanning for conflict checks instead of the O(1) sets.
- Forgetting to remove the three constraint keys on backtrack.
- Not recording the queen column in a way that allows board reconstruction at the base case.

```java
// WRONG: O(n) scan per candidate column
boolean safe = true;
for (int prevRow = 0; prevRow < row; prevRow++) {
    if (queens[prevRow] == col || Math.abs(queens[prevRow] - col) == row - prevRow) {
        safe = false;
        break;
    }
}

// CORRECT: O(1) set check
if (!cols.contains(col) && !diag1.contains(row - col) && !diag2.contains(row + col)) {
    // safe
}
```

### What about the banking domain use case?

At the National Bank of India, the constraint scheduler assigns n critical jobs to n time-server slots where no two jobs can share a resource line modeled like a queen's reach. The three-set backtracking finds all valid assignments, and the bitmask version handles larger scheduling grids efficiently.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ConstraintScheduler {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NQueensSolver solver = new NQueensSolver();
        int n = 6; // 6 jobs, 6 server slots
        int count = solver.solveNQueens(n).size();
        System.out.println("Valid schedules for " + n + " jobs: " + count);
    }
}
```

```text
Valid schedules for 6 jobs: 4
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you check diagonals in O(1)? (row - col and row + col sets.)
- What is the time complexity? (Super-polynomial but much less than n^n.)
- How many solutions for n = 8? (92.)
- Can you optimize further? (Symmetry halving, bitmask encoding.)
- How does this relate to Sudoku? (Same paradigm: constraint-satisfaction backtracking with set-based pruning.)
